A||||19||DISenchanted||Valve Cover seal||||1||
Z||000000||DISenchanted||01-26-2000||05:33 PM||||Ok, she's sprung a leak.  What am I looking at here?  As far as difficulty and cost?  Should I do it, or is there someone in Southern California that you'd suggest?<P>Let me know.<P>DISenchanted||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||01-26-2000||05:36 PM||||I haven't replaced one, but I've heard you need to pull the plenum and middle intake to get the valve cover off. This is assuming your talking about the v-6.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-26-2000||06:18 PM||||On the 4 cyl you pull the EGR to get at the one bolt under it. Otherwise it's not hard. The new cover gasket should come with a new EGR gasket. If it doesn't use a diferant kit or make sure you get a new EGR gasket.<P>Use the rubber gasket. It's a tougher one than cork and less likely to leak. Also usually easier to clean up if you ever have to have it apart again.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||01-26-2000||06:55 PM||||duh duh duh...<BR>I should have looked at your name: DISenchanted. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||AL||01-26-2000||08:52 PM||||hey dis go for it, it'snot that hard, but if your not mechanically inclined well i could do it for you. just buy the gaskets and we can work out a reasonable price. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||01-26-2000||08:57 PM||||I would say to give us a call for that, but it would so amazingly easy you would laugh. You could indeed do it yourself, it is not that hard. Dealer sells the gaskets with the metal washers as opposed to the felpro and I would recomend getting valve bolt plates....only way I can describe them are as long strips of metal that you put the bolt through and it give a larger area of preasure on the gasket instead of binding it.<P>The guys are right, save yourself money and just do it!<BR>IF its a 4 cyl!<P>If you don't want to deal with it than Fiero Motorsports number is 714 639-9005. Tell them Anthony sent you.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-26-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000006||Gokart||01-26-2000||09:15 PM||||Took mine into a shop to replace gasket and he got cover off without removing plenum/manifolds. Don't know how the heck he did it though seeing it took me two hours trying to get it off and I couldn't.||||reg||5
Z||000007||David Gamache||01-26-2000||09:38 PM||||The job maybe possible without removing the plenum, especially if the valve cover that needs replacing is the one to the rear of the car facing the trunk. simply remove some brackets and your on your way. But it saves a ton of struggle and cursing if the top of the plenum is removed for replacing the valve cover gasket on the side facing the back window. The gasket set is only about 15 bucks for the intake set, then the inside cover is much easier to remove. It is a bit of work, but can be done without a masters in enginering. ||||reg||7
Z||000008||AL||01-26-2000||11:59 PM||||hey anthony where are you guys located?   do you guys sell accesories and if so, where in corona, i'm in funtucky(fontana) AL.||||reg||1
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||01-27-2000||02:07 AM||||Fiero Motorsports is in Orange/Anaheim area not to far from the Pond on Batavia between Collins and Walnut. Make sure to give em a call, some weeks get pretty hectic. Tell them Anthony sent you. It depends on the type of accessories you are talking about. Some we can get like roller tip rockers, cams etc. But most of the other stuff we would refer you to FOCOA which isnt too far away from us. They are on Anaheim blvd east of the 5 freeway. BTW Fieros West does have meeting there the first Saturday of each month. We pull out the BBQ and burn the dogs until the sun goes down.<BR>The valve cover would not clear the rocker arms with the plenum in place, even if you could reach the bolts on the top of the cover. If anybody ever has done it with the plenum I would love to know how to do it. The plenum is not hard but a bundle of vacuum lines underneath. You have to be VERY careful to make sure that you don't have a vacuum leak when you put everything back together.<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-27-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000010||DISenchanted||01-27-2000||03:13 PM||||Sorry, I should have been more specific.  It's the rear seal on an 85gt.  It is only leaking in a fairly small area.  If it's as simple as you say, I still think that I'd rather someone else do it.  But I'd like to watch for the next time.  Al, if you think we could do it then I'm all for it.  If not then I think Fiero Motorsports will be getting a call from me.<P>Let me know.<P>DISenchanted||||reg||1
Z||000011||mwbackus||01-27-2000||06:40 PM||||According to this thread:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002027.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002027.html</A> <P>"mrfiero" has removed the valve covers without removal of the intake plenum!  Perhaps he could better describe his method for us?  It could be very valuble information to those of us who have attempted this proceedure in the past, and to those that need frequent readjustments of valve lash on the V6.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Mike||||reg||1
